ne on the basis of cross-party  consensus because it's got to stand the  test of time  and it's all very easy for one side or  another to come up with with some a  great idea and but you have to go  through the details so  just as a for instance uh there is a  suggestion at the moment that um the  um that you should limit the number of  hours an mp can spend on uh  non-parliamentary duties  well i don't know what counts as a  non-parliamentary duty does that include  knocking doors in an election for  instance or not i don't know which side  of the  of the equation that falls um  what what do you say about traveling  between your constituency in london is  that part of your parliamentary duty or  or not and and more importantly how on  earth would the commissioner ever  investigate whether somebody had spent  more hours than they should or they  shouldn't on their parliamentary duties  of course every mp  should throw themselves heart and soul  into the business of being an mp and and  that's what all of us do i think i mean  um my day i don't want  you won't feel sorry for me i know  because you start very very early in the  morning but um my day yesterday started  at six in the morning and i finished  work around about five minutes to  midnight last night and that is not  uncommon for mvs um so the the whole  idea that  which i think dominic rob was espousing  there and and others were yesterday on  behalf of the government that it's going  to be very easy just to come up with  some plan in the next few days  which says um you can do so many hours  doing this and so many hours doing that  it's just  for the birds frankly  um starts at six o'clock in the morning  you've already missed an hour of the  early rundown by then i'm going to tell  neil patterson of you now let's talk  about owen patterson should we in a bit  more detail he broke the rules it has  been accepted by organizing a meeting  between the health minister and randox  doesn't seem to be any minutes 